bangladesh's economy has been one of the  fastest growing in the world but in  recent months soaring energy and food  prices have inflated its import bill  forcing the government to seek loans  from global agencies and raise fuel  prices the impact is already been felt  by many here in the capital dhaka  covered van owner mohammed shah jahan is  worried about the future  no one is renting our vans right now  because they cost more  this is really hard on us you can see  all the drivers sitting idle we can't  figure out what the government is doing  sporadic protests are taking place  nearly every day but security forces are  cracking down this is the second time  the government has increased fuel prices  in just about nine months  the government has blamed russia's war  on ukraine for the increase however  economists have won this would led to  higher inflation and forced people to  cut down on their spending  the rise in food prices is becoming a  concern for people with limited earnings  prices for all food items have gone up  since the hike in fuel prices as  everything has to be transported but our  income has not increased accordingly  transport costs have also gone up in  recent days  the bus fare has considerably gone up  and it's hard on us but what can we do  we have to manage hopefully the  government will bring it under control  soon  economists say the impact of more than  50 increase in fuel prices will affect  the poor and fixed income people the  most this  fuel price is going to have an impact  on  various sections of the economy at least  at three levels  firstly through high transport cost  secondly  there will be increased cost to  production and  in the end  these are going to have an impact on the  consumer  bangladesh's economy has barely  recovered from the pandemic while  thousands of families are now at risk of  being poor again because of the sudden  rise in expenses  experts 1 the nation is facing an  uncertain economic future sandvik  choudhary al-jazeera dhaka
 

